WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.860
Hey, welcome to fascinating frontiers, episode 70, coming to you on May 14, 2026.

00:00:06.640 --> 00:00:12.460
The universe has been busy, let's get into it. A faint halo surrounds the eclipse sun in Orion's

00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:18.040
spacecraft images, hinting at light behavior no one fully expected in the vacuum of space.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:25.000
Perseverance assembled 61 frames into a self-portrait that shows a fresh abrasion patch on the rock

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:31.200
named Arthusa. The image marks the farthest west the rover has traveled beyond Gezero crater

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:37.020
during its current campaign. The scene comes from the fifth science campaign where the rover is

00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:43.740
systematically sampling rocks that may record Mars' wetter past. 61 individual frames were

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:49.260
stitched into an animated selfie that shows both the abrasion patch and the sweeping ancient terrain

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:55.320
behind it. The detailed documentation helps correlate surface textures with mineral data collected

00:00:55.320 --> 00:01:00.580
by the rover instruments. Now shifting to an unexpected view captured closer to home.

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:06.760
An unexpected ring of light encircles the moon during a solar eclipse captured from the Orion

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:12.160
spacecraft. The feature remains visible even where the moon blocks direct sunlight.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.020
The halo rules out simple camera or atmospheric effects according to the images released from

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:23.820
the mission. This observation came during a solar eclipse viewed directly from the spacecraft in

00:01:23.820 --> 00:01:29.600
orbit. Next up is a look at particles that travel across vast distances before reaching us.

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:35.740
Ultra-high energy particles continue to strike Earth after traveling from distant cosmic accelerators.

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:40.500
If you lined up every proton that hits Earth atmosphere in a single second,

00:01:40.840 --> 00:01:46.320
their combined energy would rival a small nuclear blast spread across the entire planet.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.960
These particles race in a nearly the speed of light after being flung from supernova remnants,

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:57.240
black hole jets, or distant active galaxies. Magnetic fields scramble their

00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:02.360
arrival directions like river currents deflecting floating leaves, erasing any straight line memory

00:02:02.360 --> 00:02:08.640
of where they started. One rare event carries the energy of a well-thrown baseball packed into

00:02:08.640 --> 00:02:14.660
a single subatomic particle. Detectors on the ground and in orbit record arrival times and energies

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:20.080
yet the exact launch sites remain hidden because the deflections are too complex to reverse.

00:02:20.980 --> 00:02:26.100
Scientists still cannot explain why the very highest energy rays appear to come from directions

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:31.920
that do not match any known powerful objects in the sky. On a different note, the crescent moon

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.140
offers an easy way to find Mars this week. The crescent moon sits just seven degrees from Mars in

00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:44.120
the pre-dawn eastern sky. This pairing offers a simple visual marker for locating the red planet

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:49.800
before sunrise. The alignment is visible to anyone willing to step outside before sunrise.

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:56.200
Now turning to an amateur capture of a distant nebula. A new amateur image reveals intricate dark

00:02:56.200 --> 00:03:01.680
lanes and bright emission within the elephant trunk nebula after 400 minutes of integration.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:07.320
The result came from a compact smart telescope operating under suburban skies.

00:03:07.980 --> 00:03:13.520
The exposure demonstrates how accessible instruments can now reach complex deep sky targets

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:19.480
without traveling to dark sites. Engineers are examining concepts that could change how we reach

00:03:19.480 --> 00:03:25.500
orbit. Recent design studies examine tether materials and climber systems capable of reaching

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:31.740
geostationary orbit. The approach could reduce launch costs dramatically if structural and safety

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:37.380
hurdles are solved. Work has moved from theory to practical engineering questions around the tether

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:43.820
and climber systems. Shifting inward to the center of our own galaxy, a six hour exposure

00:03:43.820 --> 00:03:50.880
through an astromodified DSLR reveals ionized hydrogen clouds and dust lanes around Sagittarius A star.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:56.640
The image separates emission structures from the dense stellar background in the Milky Way core.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.200
The long integration captures both h alpha and continuum light to highlight these features.

00:04:03.000 --> 00:04:07.180
Meanwhile, a planetarium program is bringing recent missions to new audiences.

00:04:07.760 --> 00:04:13.960
The Hong Kong Space Museum opens a new planetarium program focused on the Shinzo 13 mission.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:19.380
The show highlights crew activities and orbital views for public audiences starting tomorrow.

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:25.080
The program expands access to stories from Chinese spaceflight for general viewers.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:29.580
Finally, early contributors to American rocketry reflect on the field.

00:04:30.300 --> 00:04:35.940
Early contributors to American rocketry and aviation reflect on expanding access to spaceflight.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:41.040
Their perspectives emphasize both technical progress and the need for broader participation

00:04:41.040 --> 00:04:46.520
in future programs. Next time, keep an eye on further updates from the perseverance rover

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:52.140
as it continues sampling along the crater rim. That covers today's space and science news.

00:04:52.860 --> 00:04:57.700
Share this with a fellow space enthusiast if you found it interesting. I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:05:02.600
See you tomorrow. This episode used AI voice synthesis of my voice.

00:05:02.920 --> 00:05:05.360
Editorial selection and analysis are my own.
